From c14f81c330d2e601a5d2a6bc020530d61febe3c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Silas=20M=C3=BCller?= <github@silasmueller.de>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 09:47:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nvidia, nvidia-modeset: hold a video memory reserve for
 scanout surfaces
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A scanout surface is physically contiguous video memory with no fallback:
nvidia-drm falls back to system memory only for NO_SCANOUT buffers.  A
process that sizes its pools to the free video memory, such as a game
sizing them to the Vulkan memory budget, can leave no room for the next
surface.  The refused allocation leaves Xwayland or the compositor without
a buffer and the display stops updating.

Let the display driver declare a reserve.  NVKMS knows the committed modes:
after every successful modeset it computes, per active head, five XRGB8888
surfaces of the head's ViewPortIn and four cursors of the maximum cursor
size, and sends the sum and the largest surface size to RM with the new
kernel-privileged control NV0073_CTRL_CMD_SYSTEM_SET_DISPLAY_RESERVE.  It
declares zero when it tears down its displays.  The nvidia-modeset
parameter display_reserve_mib selects this (-1), a fixed size in MiB, or
nothing (0, the default).

RM holds the reserve as runs of PMA pages, one per surface with the page
size RM gives such a surface, plus one run for the remainder.  The runs
leave PMA's free count, so processes see less free video memory; they are
not RM objects and are not charged to any cgroup.  When PMA refuses a
scanout allocation of a kernel client (kernel privilege, ISO, physically
contiguous, not NO_SCANOUT) in _vidmemPmaAllocate, runs that cover it are
freed and the allocation is retried.  Allocations through the RM API hold
the API lock for writing unless RmRoApiLock enables read-only allocations,
so no other such allocation can take the freed pages first; UVM allocates
from PMA directly and may, and then the next run is released.

A release schedules a refill one second later, and a refill that falls
short retries on a backoff of one to thirty seconds.  A new declaration
re-cuts the reserve by allocating the new runs before freeing the old ones.
Refills run as a work item with the API lock and without the GPU lock, and
only at full GPU power with PMA initialized and MIG disabled.  The runs are
freed in memmgrStatePreUnload, before unload, suspend and GC6/RTD3 entry,
and refilled from memmgrStatePostLoad.  They are never mapped or written,
so they are freed without scrubbing.

Signed-off-by: Silas Müller <github@silasmueller.de>
